      "feedback": "Your draft covers the incredible biology and behavior of the western honey bee beautifully, but we need to pump the brakes on the IUCN classification before this goes live. There is a persistent botanical/zoological myth floating around that honey bees are \"Extinct in the Wild,\" but that isn't the case!\n\nHere is the breakdown of the status updates and how to settle the two flagged fields to keep your signage factually bulletproof.\n\n🔍 Debunking the \"Extinct in the Wild\" Status\nWhile your instinct to protect the profile from sounding overly alarmist is spot-on, the official IUCN Red List standing is quite different:\n\nThe Actual Listing: For a long time, the IUCN listed the wild western honey bee (Apis mellifera) as Data Deficient. However, a major reassessment by European scientists officially moved wild EU populations to Endangered due to severe colony declines from habitat loss and Varroa mites.\n\nThe Global Reality: They are nowhere near \"Extinct in the Wild.\" Free-living, feral coloni",
